d = json.load(open(p))
d["objectives"] = [
 "State the Boolean algebra laws and apply De Morgan's laws as rewrite rules to simplify compound conditions.",
 "Compute bitwise AND/OR/XOR/NOT and shifts by hand on 8-bit values.",
 "Test, set, clear, and toggle bit flags; decode Unix permission bitmasks.",
 "Derive network addresses with bitwise AND on non-octet-boundary masks.",
 "Encrypt and decrypt with XOR, and explain why key reuse breaks stream ciphers.",
 "Diagnose signedness, width, and precedence gotchas in bitwise code.",
]
exts = [
 "There is a meta-pattern worth naming: duality. Every Boolean law has a dual obtained by swapping AND with OR and 0 with 1 — absorption's A OR (A AND B) = A duals to A AND (A OR B) = A, and De Morgan's laws are duals of each other. When you prove or learn one form, you get the other free. Use duality as a bug detector: if a simplification looks right in one form, check its dual — if the dual looks wrong, recheck the original. And treat the laws as refactoring rules for conditions, the way you would refactor code: the goal is always fewer literals and clearer intent. A condition like (isAdmin AND inVPN) OR (isAdmin AND NOT inVPN) OR (NOT isAdmin AND inVPN) should trigger the same instinct as a duplicated code block — simplify to isAdmin OR inVPN and delete the cleverness. Reviewers should flag unsimplified conditions the way they flag duplicated code: not because they are wrong, but because the next person to touch them will make them wrong. Simplicity is not aesthetic here; it is the difference between a condition you can audit and one you merely hope about.",
 "Detection engineers meet De Morgan in SIEM rules constantly. A rule like 'alert when NOT (known_good_process AND expected_parent)' must be written as 'alert when (NOT known_good_process) OR (NOT expected_parent)' — and the number of detection rules that get this wrong in production is nonzero and embarrassing. The failure mode is silent: the buggy AND version under-alerts (it only fires when both are anomalous), so nobody notices the gap until the incident review asks why the rule did not fire. Defensive practice: write detection conditions in negation normal form (negations pushed to literals) as a team standard, and add a review checklist item for every NOT spanning a compound. Better yet, express the positive case and negate once at the end: define is_expected = known_good AND expected_parent, then alert on NOT is_expected — one negation, no distribution, no De Morgan bug possible. The broader lesson from this note: every manual application of De Morgan is a chance to err, so structure conditions to minimize the applications. Let the algebra do the work once, at design time, not in the analyst's head at 3 AM.",
 "The operator payoff for bitwise fluency is reading hex dumps and protocol fields without reaching for a calculator. A TCP flags byte of 0x12: 0x12 = 0001 0010 — SYN (0x02) and ACK (0x10) set: it is a SYN-ACK. You read that by knowing 0x10 = 16 = 00010000 and 0x02 = 00000010 and ORing them mentally. IP header 0x45: version 4 (high nibble 0100), IHL 5 (low nibble 0101, 5x4 = 20 byte header). These are mask-and-shift operations done by eye, and they turn packet captures from opaque hex into readable structure. Practice: take any pcap, decode the TCP flags byte of three packets by hand, then verify with Wireshark's decode. The second payoff is subnetting speed: 192.168.1.130/26 gives network .128 should eventually be mental arithmetic (130 AND 192: 130 is 128+2, 192 keeps the 128, drops the 2, result 128). Fluency here is not trivia — it is the difference between reading the network and guessing at it, and in incident response that difference is measured in minutes.",
 "There is a security-theoretic framing that makes bitmasks more than a trick: they are capabilities. A capability is an unforgeable token of authority, and a bitmask is the simplest capability system — each bit a permission, the integer a bundle of rights. The confused-deputy problem lives here: a program holding a bitmask with more bits than it needs for the current task can be tricked into exercising the excess authority. Principle of least privilege, stated in bits: at any moment, hold the minimal mask for the task — set bits late, clear them early. This is also why 'default deny' and bitmasks rhyme: an empty mask denies everything, and every granted bit is an explicit decision. When you audit a system, ask for its bitmasks: the permission mask on files, the capability mask on processes, the feature-flag mask on the application. Overly broad masks are the bit-level signature of excessive privilege, and they are checkable mechanically — which is more than you can say for most privilege problems. Bits do not lie about what they permit; people just forget to read them.",
 "The router's real algorithm generalizes the single AND: longest-prefix match. A router holds many (prefix, mask, next-hop) entries; for each destination it ANDs the address with every mask, keeps the matches, and forwards via the longest matching prefix (most specific wins). Worked: entries 10.0.0.0/8 to A, 10.5.0.0/16 to B, 10.5.8.0/22 to C. Destination 10.5.9.200: AND with /8 gives 10.0.0.0 (match); with /16 gives 10.5.0.0 (match); with /22 gives 10.5.8.0 (match, computed in the note). Longest match: /22, forward via C. This is why route tables can hold overlapping entries without conflict — specificity resolves it deterministically. The security angle: route injection attacks (BGP hijacks) work by announcing more-specific prefixes, exploiting longest-match to steal traffic. Defenses (RPKI, prefix filters) are about constraining which specifics you will accept. And the same longest-match logic appears in firewall rules with CIDR sources: overlapping allow and deny entries resolve by specificity in well-designed systems — know your platform's resolution rule, because 'most specific wins' versus 'first match wins' produce different networks from the same rules.",
 "XOR's other great role is integrity, not secrecy: checksums and hash functions use XOR folding to compress data into check values. The parity bit (XOR of all data bits) detects any single-bit flip; CRCs generalize this with polynomial division; hash functions mix with XOR-heavy rounds. The property that makes XOR right for integrity is diffusion of differences: flipping one input bit flips the XOR result predictably, so tampering shows. But note the asymmetry with secrecy: XOR gives no integrity in encryption (flipping a ciphertext bit flips the plaintext bit predictably — malleability), and checksums give no secrecy. The classic failure is using one where the other is needed: a CRC 'protecting' a firmware image against malicious modification (the attacker recomputes the CRC), or XOR 'encryption' without a MAC (the attacker mauls the plaintext). The rule: secrecy needs a keyed, non-malleable construction; integrity needs a keyed MAC (or a hash in a tamper-evident context). XOR is the humble building block inside both — and the source of vivid failures whenever it is mistaken for the whole building.",
 "Test bitwise code with properties, not just examples, because the edge cases are where the bugs live. The key properties: idempotence of masks ((x AND mask) AND mask equals x AND mask); round-trip of set and clear (clearing a set flag drops it); XOR self-inverse (x XOR k XOR k equals x); shift-multiply equivalence (x shifted left n equals x times 2 to the n for non-negative x within width). Write these as automated property tests with random inputs across the full width including 0, all-ones, and the sign boundary — example-only tests ('it worked for 0b11010111') miss the width and sign edges where C and Python diverge. Specifically test: the maximum value (0xFF for 8-bit), values with the top bit set (signedness!), shift counts of 0 and width-1, and in Python, values that would overflow a fixed width (to catch missing AND-0xFF masks). Fuzz the C version against the Python reference implementation: same inputs, same outputs, or a documented reason. Bitwise code is small enough to verify exhaustively over 8 or 16 bits — do it. A 65,536-iteration exhaustive test runs in milliseconds and proves more than a hundred hand-picked examples.",
]
